# Conversion Funnel Rules — The Growth Strategist

Read `00-brand-core.mdc` first. This site is the conversion stage of a funnel that starts on
Instagram/YouTube. Cold social traffic has almost no patience — every section has to earn the
next scroll.

## The napkin test

Before adding any new section, state in one sentence which rung of the ladder it serves. If you
can't, it doesn't belong on the page yet.

## The ladder (canonical section order — do not flatten into unordered "features")

1. Hook (video) — sets up the "you vs you" framing, ends on a line that only resolves on the site
2. Identity match — "your business isn't sinking, it's not going anywhere" — must land in the
   first 3 seconds of scroll or the visitor bounces
3. Agitation — the canonical letter
4. Mechanism — boosting vs. campaign contrast
5. Proof — the anti-testimonial stats (the "$0 vs $270,000" honesty flex is the strongest single
   asset on the page — never dilute it with normal social proof language)
6. Offer stack — present explicitly as a ladder being climbed, not three parallel options
7. Scarcity — 2 free slots / gatekeeping copy
8. Objection handling — FAQ
9. Last call — hardest line on the page, single CTA, no other navigation competing for attention

## CTA copy must change with scroll depth

Never reuse the same CTA label at every section. The button label should shift with the
psychological distance already covered:

- Top of page: "Watch the truth" / "See the gap"
- Mid-page: "Show me what I'm doing wrong"
- Near pricing: "Prove us wrong"
- Bottom: "Stop being fine" / "Apply for the free slot"

## Friction rules (Hormozi value equation: maximize dream outcome + belief, minimize time + effort)

- The free-slot application must be the lowest-friction action on the page — fewest fields,
  fastest response, no gate before it. This is the top of the paid ladder's on-ramp; cold
  traffic will not tolerate friction here.
- Paid tiers can carry more friction (booking a call, multi-step form) because a visitor
  reaching that point has already climbed rungs 1–6.
- Never add a popup, modal, or interstitial before rung 3 (agitation). Earn attention before
  interrupting it.

## Scarcity and gatekeeping

Scarcity copy ("2 businesses a month," "we pick you") must stay literal and specific, never
vague urgency ("limited time!" "act now!"). Vague urgency reads as generic marketing and breaks
the brand's credibility.
